WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Process Improvement and the Role of Measurement

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.433
Welcome back to this session on the power of measurement. As you can see,

3
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:17.399
I have a simple little line marked here, with beginning point and an end point.

4
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:24.366
My question is, I’d like for you to describe this line for me.

5
00:00:24.366 --> 00:00:31.399
Think of a way to describe it without numbers, without measurements.

6
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:36.333
My, it becomes a difficult task.

7
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:43.333
One naturally and intuitively says, well how long is the line?

8
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:48.766
This is why we have rulers.

9
00:00:48.766 --> 00:00:54.366
See we have a standard inch. And I can measure this line.

10
00:00:54.366 --> 00:00:58.566
And if I do so, it looks to me to be

11
00:00:58.566 --> 00:01:05.266
three and a quarter inch.

12
00:01:05.266 --> 00:01:11.232
Let's really tighten in on this point right here.

13
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:14.666
Right there, we’re gonna really tighen in on that. Okay,

14
00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:20.299
we can see that it's three and a quarter inches wide.

15
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.300
three and one quarter inch.

16
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:30.700
Now, we know that a line is the shortest distance between two points.

17
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:37.666
And we described that line in a standard inch. See this 1 inch is a standard.

18
00:01:37.666 --> 00:01:41.699
There sits an inch over in a vault in England.

19
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:47.466
That's the standard from which all rulers and everything else is based.

20
00:01:47.466 --> 00:01:52.799
And of course, the inch is arbitrary. It could've been a googlelog, whatever that may be.

21
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.766
But nonetheless, we have a measurement here, three and a quarter inches.

22
00:01:58.766 --> 00:02:04.232
And that measurement. You know there is a simple saying that we’ve got.

23
00:02:04.233 --> 00:02:09.166
Let the data

24
00:02:09.166 --> 00:02:13.166
do the talking.

25
00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:18.266
I can't over emphasize the power of that thought.

26
00:02:18.266 --> 00:02:25.232
Let the data do the talking because, you know, data speaks very loud.

27
00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:31.833
Data is management by fact.

28
00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:37.066
Management by fact.

29
00:02:37.066 --> 00:02:40.899
We don't manage by circumstance. We don't manage by opinion.

30
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.466
We don't manage by the seat of our pants. We manage by fact.

31
00:02:44.466 --> 00:02:49.299
And let me tell you, fact equals data.

32
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:54.400
Because it's verifiable. It’s repeatable. Recall, we talked about verifiable knowledge.

33
00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.300
So, three and a quarter inches is verifiable knowledge.

34
00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.866
When I asked for a description about this line, it’s verifiable.

35
00:03:05.866 --> 00:03:10.566
This person here, can take this ruler,

36
00:03:10.566 --> 00:03:13.732
and verify that line.

37
00:03:13.733 --> 00:03:16.733
So, can the next person and the next person.

38
00:03:16.733 --> 00:03:19.733
So, that when we all say it's three and a quarter inches.

39
00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:24.599
You can see there's little variation between people.

40
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:31.600
So, these people all have the same knowledge.

41
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:37.733
That's what's great about measurements. They all carry the same knowledge.

42
00:03:37.733 --> 00:03:41.533
Where again, if you didn't have this, if that was unknown,

43
00:03:41.533 --> 00:03:45.399
then the knowledge about that line would be different.

44
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.966
Far greater difference, between these three individuals,

45
00:03:48.966 --> 00:03:52.932
than if we did have the measurements.  So again, we let the data do the talking.

46
00:03:52.933 --> 00:03:58.899
Management by fact. So simply recall, once again.

47
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:05.500
At the risk of beating a dead horse to death, and that's okay in this case.

48
00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:11.133
Because it's a really valuable horse. Y is a function of X.

49
00:04:11.133 --> 00:04:17.766
If we can't measure Y and we can't measure X, then the actions that we take,

50
00:04:17.766 --> 00:04:25.066
the knobs in our processes that we go twist. How do we know the effect that that has on Y?

51
00:04:25.066 --> 00:04:30.066
Unless, here's Y, a scale of Y,

52
00:04:30.066 --> 00:04:37.066
and here's the range of operation of Y, before we twist knob X.

53
00:04:37.066 --> 00:04:43.399
So, this is before. See, and this is after.

54
00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.733
Well notice there is some overlap here. Yeah, that overlap represents uncertainty.

55
00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:51.733
I don't know whether twisting knob X.

56
00:04:51.733 --> 00:04:56.066
If I see a data point occur there. X is the reason for that or not.

57
00:04:56.066 --> 00:04:59.099
The more that these two separate,

58
00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.766
the more certainty I have that X is the causal variable with respect to Y.

59
00:05:01.766 --> 00:05:06.066
the more certainty I have that X is the causal variable with respect to Y.

60
00:05:06.066 --> 00:05:12.466
But see once again, unless I can quantify Y.

61
00:05:12.466 --> 00:05:18.599
And say what the measurements of Y are, for each occurrence of Y.

62
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:24.833
Unless I let the data do the talking. Unless I manage the situation by fact, by data.

63
00:05:24.833 --> 00:05:30.266
Then all I have is intuitive, experiential, knowledge

64
00:05:30.266 --> 00:05:36.966
of the effect of X on Y. So we want to scientifically

65
00:05:36.966 --> 00:05:41.766
be able to identify the vital few X’s out there.

66
00:05:41.766 --> 00:05:48.766
And what their consequential impact is on Y, so I can prioritize them for process control reasons.

67
00:05:48.766 --> 00:05:55.199
That's why this idea of measurement

68
00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.733
is so important. And why the idea,

69
00:06:00.733 --> 00:06:06.566
of let the data do the talking for the product or the service. Doesn't matter.

70
00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:10.899
Measurements are measurements, they apply equally.

71
00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:15.566
It's an equal opportunity employer so to speak.

72
00:06:15.566 --> 00:06:19.732
The numbers don't care whether they come from a service process, or

73
00:06:19.733 --> 00:06:23.033
from a commercial process. Measurements are measurements.

74
00:06:23.033 --> 00:06:26.333
And that's why they're so important.

75
00:06:26.333 --> 00:06:31.333
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
